  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nDamp and dreary conditions continue across the region this afternoon \nas low pressure continues to lift to the northeast, into western \nIowa and Minnesota. Rainfall will gradually end from southwest to \nnortheast through the course of the afternoon into early evening. A \nmid-level trough dives southeast across eastern Nebraska/western \nIowa overnight with strong north winds arriving. Winds gusting to \naround 40-45mph will be possible overnight and into early Friday \nmorning. \n\nWinds will gradually decrease Friday morning/afternoon as the trough \ncontinues to dive toward the Ozarks. Expect a cool but drier day \nwith highs reaching the low to mid-50s. Friday night/Saturday brings \nsome concerns for the potential for the first frost/freeze event of \nthe season for our area. Clearing skies and light north winds will \nbe favorable for temperatures taking a plunge overnight. Forecasted \nlows are currently expected to be in the low 30s, which prompted the \nissuance of a Freeze Watch for the CWA for Friday night/Saturday \nmorning. One thing to watch, particularly for the southeastern \nportion of the area will be how quickly cloud cover moves out of the \nregion. Current forecasted lows along the \nKansas/Nebraska/Missouri/Iowa borders is expected to fall to around \n30 or 31 degrees. However, should cloud cover linger slightly longer \nthan currently anticipated in this area, this could keep our \ntemperatures slightly warmer. Those with agricultural interests will \nwant to continue to monitor forecast trends heading into tomorrow \nnight.\n\nA ridge over the Rockies will shift east over the weekend. Expect a \ngradual warming trend through early next week as warmer air moves \nin. Saturday highs will be in the upper 50s to low 60s, while \nTuesday highs are currently forecasted for the low to mid-70s. After \ntoday's rainfall, dry conditions are expected to persist through mid-\nweek.",
